# ArcCross competitive research brief

**Research date:** 28 October 2026  
**Scope:** macOS, Windows, Linux, and Android device connection, especially file transfer and broader phone-to-computer integration.

## Executive summary

ArcCross is entering an established category, not an empty one. LocalSend already offers no-account, local-network file transfer across all four target platforms. KDE Connect has a broader feature set—clipboard, notifications, file browsing, and remote input—with clients for those systems, though feature parity varies. PairDrop removes the install requirement through a browser and can transfer over the internet. OPPO/OnePlus' O+ Connect goes further into remote control and screen sharing, but its phone side depends on supported vendor devices and software.

The strongest opportunity is therefore a **reliability and usability promise for mixed-device households**, especially where a Mac, Windows PC, Linux machine, and Android phone need to work together. “Cross-platform file sharing” alone is not a defensible gap. ArcCross would need to make discovery, pairing, transfer recovery, and platform-specific permissions feel dependable and explainable.

The recurring engineering trap is treating “both devices are on Wi-Fi” as proof that they can find and reach one another. Multicast discovery, firewall rules, VPNs, guest-network isolation, Android local-network permissions, and OS background limits can each break a different step. The app should identify which step failed and offer a recovery path.

The second trap is committing to a wide feature set before deciding what ArcCross is for. File transfer, clipboard, notifications, remote control, screen mirroring, sync, and internet relay create different permission, security, and support burdens. Pick the first job, make it excellent on all four platforms, and add the next layer only after validating demand.

## 1. “O+ Connect” identity resolved

The likely intended product is **O+ Connect**, OPPO's cross-device software. Its official [OPPO Connect page](https://connect.oppo.com/) says O+ Connect has been renamed to OPPO Connect. The related [OnePlus Connect page](https://connect.oneplus.com/) uses the same product name and advertises file transfer, device file management, remote PC control, phone screen casting, keyboard/mouse sharing, PC screen extension, notification sync, and clipboard sync.

The official [OnePlus OxygenOS 16 page](https://www.oneplus.com/us/oxygenos16) says the PC experience requires the O+ Connect app on macOS 10.14+ or Windows 10+, and says some remote-control capability is currently limited to OnePlus Pad 3. These are product-family integrations: support and feature availability depend on phone/tablet model and software. OPPO's [Mac connection guide](https://support.oppo.com/lk/answer/?aid=2215536) lists a ColorOS version prerequisite for its described workflow. The official [privacy notice](https://connect.oppo.com/privacy/oshare_en.html) also documents permission prompts and says its iOS app may need to remain in the foreground for a photo/video transfer to continue normally after it leaves the foreground.

There is a similarly named but unrelated **O-Connect**: the [Google Play listing](https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.onpassive.oconnect) identifies it as ONPASSIVE's messaging and video-conferencing app. It is not the OPPO/OnePlus device-connectivity product.

## 2. Competitive matrix

“All four” means a supported client or browser experience on macOS, Windows, Linux, and Android. A product's platform listing does not mean every feature behaves identically on every OS.

| Product | Main job and target-platform coverage | What it does well | Documented boundary or risk |
|---|---|---|---|
| **LocalSend** | Nearby file/message transfer; native apps for **macOS, Windows, Linux, Android** (also iOS). | Open source, no account, local-network transfers, HTTPS-based protocol, simple accept/send flow. This is ArcCross's closest direct competitor if its first release is file sharing. ([Project README](https://github.com/localsend/localsend), [protocol](https://github.com/localsend/protocol/blob/main/README.md)) | Same-LAN reachability is not guaranteed. The maintainer's discovery issue lists AP isolation, subnet size, configuration, and firewall/network issues; a macOS/Android report shows discovery can be one-way even when both apps are present. ([#527](https://github.com/localsend/localsend/issues/527), [#2371](https://github.com/localsend/localsend/issues/2371)) |
| **KDE Connect** | General phone–computer integration; current KDE download pages list **Android, macOS, Windows, Linux** (also iOS and other clients). | Clipboard, notifications, files, remote control, phone finder, and a broad plugin model. KDE lists current Windows packages and a stable macOS download. ([KDE feature page](https://apps.kde.org/kdeconnect/), [downloads](https://kdeconnect.kde.org/download.html)) | Feature parity is affected by OS policy. For example, KDE's user documentation says Android 10+ requires an explicit “Send Clipboard” action for phone-to-desktop clipboard sharing; users report notification and clipboard inconsistencies across desktop clients. ([KDE user guide](https://userbase.kde.org/KDEConnect/en), [KDE Discuss clipboard report](https://discuss.kde.org/t/clipboard-copys-intermittently-not-reliable/31362)) |
